00:15:43.532 --> 00:15:48.264
challenges that each of these
entrepreneurs faced when they were

00:15:48.264 --> 00:15:53.082
building their businesses,
you learn a lot about the way that they

00:15:53.082 --> 00:15:57.577
thought about it versus the way
that you would think about it.

00:15:57.577 --> 00:16:03.161
Because the method within
the course is The instructors

00:16:03.161 --> 00:16:07.683
will often effectively
role-play the scenarios out.

00:16:07.683 --> 00:16:14.235
And it teaches you a lot about
effectively good ways that work and

00:16:14.235 --> 00:16:20.682
ways that don't work for
handling difficult conversations.

00:16:20.682 --> 00:16:22.933
And other kind of like personnel and

00:16:22.933 --> 00:16:28.260
sometimes strategic related things that
come up just on a day to day basis.

00:16:28.260 --> 00:16:32.944
And while the focus is
a lot on either startups or

00:16:32.944 --> 00:16:38.215
executive level stuff,
you end up with a perspective

00:16:38.215 --> 00:16:42.445
that cuts across lots of different areas.

00:16:42.445 --> 00:16:48.448
And you can apply it even immediately
after GSB, it doesn't have any relevance,

00:16:48.448 --> 00:16:53.500
it doesn't strictly have relevance
to things five, ten years out.

00:16:53.500 --> 00:16:57.412
So for
me that was the most impactful of course.

00:16:57.412 --> 00:16:59.178
&gt;&gt; Great, thanks for sharing.

00:16:59.178 --> 00:17:02.926
Hiral, do you mind sharing a course
that impacted you or that?

00:17:02.926 --> 00:17:06.901
&gt;&gt; Yeah, definitely,
before I share I like to Bill and John's,

00:17:06.901 --> 00:17:11.776
the managing growing enterprise,
definitely something we carry for life.

00:17:11.776 --> 00:17:14.072
But for me the most memorable for

00:17:14.072 --> 00:17:19.176
that one was because I role played to
fire someone as a board member, and

00:17:19.176 --> 00:17:24.640
that someone who is a CEO Chairman,
I was working for him three years later.

00:17:24.640 --> 00:17:29.671
Just imagine the situation, you fired
the person in the class to not be the CEO,

00:17:29.671 --> 00:17:33.141
but he still is of course,
and [LAUGH] now I'm working,

00:17:33.141 --> 00:17:33.992
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:17:33.992 --> 00:17:35.321
&gt;&gt; This is Kevin he really teaches at

00:17:35.321 --> 00:17:35.988
the GSB as well.

00:17:35.988 --> 00:17:41.025
But for me like one of my most favorite
class, there are definitely several but

00:17:41.025 --> 00:17:45.701
there's a class I took the last quarter
was called Art of Self Coaching.

00:17:45.701 --> 00:17:50.991
It was with Coach Khaled which I think
he teaches a lot of different things.

00:17:50.991 --> 00:17:55.774
But it was like it helped me sum
up my GSB experience a lot and

00:17:55.774 --> 00:18:01.936
how to carry that forward and continue
to have the learning even after GSB.

00:18:01.936 --> 00:18:06.200
So he went a lot into just happiness and
reflection and

00:18:06.200 --> 00:18:09.911
thinking about it, which Julie summarized.

00:18:09.911 --> 00:18:11.630
And I still follow his blog a lot and

00:18:11.630 --> 00:18:14.480
from time to time ask him a lot
of situational questions.

00:18:14.480 --> 00:18:22.720
He's a coach to a lot of executives
at startups and tech companies.

00:18:22.720 --> 00:18:27.077
But for me that was the most reflective
class that helped me kind of put my entire

00:18:27.077 --> 00:18:31.516
experience together, so highly encourage
when people get in to take that one.

00:18:31.516 --> 00:18:32.657
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:18:32.657 --> 00:18:34.661
Emily do you mind sharing yours?

00:18:34.661 --> 00:18:39.092
&gt;&gt; Sure, someone has to do it and
I'll do it.

00:18:39.092 --> 00:18:44.911
My really impactful class
was Interpersonal Dynamics.

00:18:44.911 --> 00:18:48.077
Touchy feely, I think it's called
interpersonal dynamics, right?

00:18:48.077 --> 00:18:49.117
&gt;&gt; It is, I know, yeah.

00:18:49.117 --> 00:18:52.951
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's what I remembered it as.

00:18:52.951 --> 00:18:55.593
I honestly like all classes like that,

00:18:55.593 --> 00:18:58.967
those are the classes that
I really appreciated.

00:18:58.967 --> 00:19:02.235
So high performance leadership,

00:19:02.235 --> 00:19:06.636
things like coaching
all my fellows classes.

00:19:06.636 --> 00:19:11.752
I think because so much of why I came
to business school was to work on my

00:19:11.752 --> 00:19:17.502
own confidence, and work on my belief
that I belonged in the business world.

00:19:17.502 --> 00:19:22.319
And so much of what those classes
accomplished for me was a real space and

00:19:22.319 --> 00:19:26.668
time to deeply understand myself and
that's such a rare thing.

00:19:26.668 --> 00:19:31.638
And such as kind of selfish thing
in some ways to be able to spend

00:19:31.638 --> 00:19:34.763
two years understanding that stuff.

00:19:38.091 --> 00:19:43.038
And I think now as a result of those
classes, I don't remember a lot of

00:19:43.038 --> 00:19:48.690
the like really specifics of the finance
classes I took or anything like that.

00:19:48.690 --> 00:19:51.738
I'm sure there's going to be another great
example from other people with that.

00:19:51.738 --> 00:19:55.929
But for me those classes are why
I'm the leader I am now today in

00:19:55.929 --> 00:20:00.038
the organizations I'm in because
I know who I am, because,

00:20:00.038 --> 00:20:04.731
I know what I want other people to
feel like when I interact with them.

00:20:04.731 --> 00:20:09.094
I'm cool with the things
that make me emotional and

00:20:09.094 --> 00:20:12.852
I'm cool with the things that make me, me.

00:20:12.852 --> 00:20:17.025
And all that stuff is like very hard,
I think,

00:20:17.025 --> 00:20:20.669
to learn or to give yourself space to do.

00:20:20.669 --> 00:20:25.496
And so I will forever be grateful for
the time to do that and

00:20:25.496 --> 00:20:28.020
have Stanford enabled that.

00:20:28.020 --> 00:20:30.620
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thanks for sharing.

00:20:30.620 --> 00:20:36.094
And Jason, is there anything else to
add about a class that impacted you?

00:20:36.094 --> 00:20:40.872
&gt;&gt; Mine is somewhat polarizing for
both current students [LAUGH] and

00:20:40.872 --> 00:20:42.649
some former students.

00:20:42.649 --> 00:20:44.782
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And even for myself, but

00:20:44.782 --> 00:20:47.324
the class that I say to this
day that I remember and

00:20:47.324 --> 00:20:50.305
think about the most was this
class called Path to Power.

00:20:50.305 --> 00:20:54.445
It talked about power and influence and
understanding your power and

00:20:54.445 --> 00:20:56.310
influence within scenarios.

00:20:56.310 --> 00:20:59.626
And I think ultimately where I came out is
how to make sure you use your power and

00:20:59.626 --> 00:21:01.884
influence for good, and
are conscious about that.

00:21:01.884 --> 00:21:06.572
And so for me, I sit in meetings all
day every day, where I'm thinking

00:21:06.572 --> 00:21:10.881
about influence and what are the power
dynamics in those rooms.

00:21:10.881 --> 00:21:14.517
And how do you make sure that
you're conscious of them and

00:21:14.517 --> 00:21:19.218
intentional about your behavior and
how it might influence decisions and

00:21:19.218 --> 00:21:22.812
how people feel, I guess,
and so using power for good.

00:21:22.812 --> 00:21:24.871
And not having power be a bad word,

00:21:24.871 --> 00:21:29.282
I guess is also something that I think
a lot of us took away from that class.

00:21:29.282 --> 00:21:31.402
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you for sharing.

00:21:31.402 --> 00:21:36.562
Yeah, I feel like the examples you
guys all gave, especially the Art of

00:21:36.562 --> 00:21:42.586
Self Coaching and Interpersonal Dynamics
and Paths to Power are also a highlight.

00:21:42.586 --> 00:21:45.556
Another thing that's really
important to the GSB experience,

00:21:45.556 --> 00:21:48.481
which is the idea of developing
yourself as a leader at the GSB.

00:21:48.481 --> 00:21:52.387
You can get schooled in finance and
strategy and

00:21:52.387 --> 00:21:56.201
things like that in a lot
of different places.

00:21:56.201 --> 00:21:59.313
But really developing yourself
as a leader to go out and

00:21:59.313 --> 00:22:03.385
manage people in organizations is
a key component of the program here.

00:22:03.385 --> 00:22:05.454
I'm going to ask one more questions and

00:22:05.454 --> 00:22:08.601
then I'll start kind of taking
them from the audience.

00:22:08.601 --> 00:22:12.302
So I thank everyone for
posting their questions, I'm seeing them.

00:22:12.302 --> 00:22:15.939
And this actually relates to one of
the ones that's been uploaded, but

00:22:15.939 --> 00:22:17.585
you guys are all five years out.

00:22:17.585 --> 00:22:22.745
And you came into the GSB with an idea
perhaps of what it is you wanted to do,

00:22:22.745 --> 00:22:27.427
maybe or at least maybe what you
wanted to get out of an MBA program.

00:22:27.427 --> 00:22:32.362
And so, just seeing where you are now,
and this is somewhat of a deep question,

00:22:32.362 --> 00:22:34.876
but are you where you thought you'd be?

00:22:34.876 --> 00:22:39.017
And for those who maybe are, that's
awesome, we'd love to hear that and for

00:22:39.017 --> 00:22:42.214
those who have pivoted or
changed, we want to hear that too.

00:22:42.214 --> 00:22:47.717
And I'd also kind of infused in that
answer if it has impacted you a bit,

00:22:47.717 --> 00:22:52.581
like what role has the Stanford
network played in helping you in

00:22:52.581 --> 00:22:54.620
your five years post GSB?

00:22:54.620 --> 00:22:57.786
So we'll go backwards now,
we'll start with Jason.

00:22:57.786 --> 00:23:01.809
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You gave me the literally, I was like,

00:23:01.809 --> 00:23:04.263
please don't start with me, [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:23:04.263 --> 00:23:05.805
&gt;&gt; But no, no, no, it works.

00:23:05.805 --> 00:23:11.132
I mean, no, I think in short, because
I don't think I knew where I would be.

00:23:11.132 --> 00:23:15.620
And I think as you do in many situations,
you get around all these

00:23:15.620 --> 00:23:20.208
