00:31:06.310 --> 00:31:08.998
I want to help bring new
robotics technologies,

00:31:08.998 --> 00:31:13.567
automation technologies into the world
in a way that's actually meaningful and

00:31:13.567 --> 00:31:15.865
can provide value, who's doing that?

00:31:15.865 --> 00:31:19.126
So there's as in the Boston area and

00:31:19.126 --> 00:31:24.671
there's a bunch of robotics
companies in the Boston area and

00:31:24.671 --> 00:31:29.799
I knew a few folks out them
from other periods in my life.

00:31:29.799 --> 00:31:35.325
But I actually found that there was a GSB
alum on the board of Berkshire Grey and I

00:31:35.325 --> 00:31:40.883
just reached out to him and he immediately
got me in conversation with the CEO.

00:31:40.883 --> 00:31:45.529
So those sorts of tactical things,
that's how the GSB

00:31:45.529 --> 00:31:50.680
alumni network can be extremely
helpful in that regard but,

00:31:50.680 --> 00:31:55.347
at a more personal level I
rely on my GSB friends for

00:31:55.347 --> 00:31:59.010
advice all the time similar
to what Emily mentioned.

00:32:00.320 --> 00:32:03.330
It's a really great support system.

00:32:03.330 --> 00:32:06.721
Folks who actually, they might not
be in your direct industry, but

00:32:06.721 --> 00:32:10.232
there's lots of corollaries and
they might invest in your industry,

00:32:10.232 --> 00:32:13.227
they might have consulted for
companies in your industry.

00:32:13.227 --> 00:32:18.583
There's just so many different angles of
attack that you have with your friends and

00:32:18.583 --> 00:32:23.555
colleagues and through the GSB network
that you get to develop a really strong

00:32:23.555 --> 00:32:28.560
perspective on a space before you even
enter it and that's a huge advantage.

00:32:30.180 --> 00:32:31.402
&gt;&gt; Thank you for sharing.

00:32:31.402 --> 00:32:36.093
John, while I have you,
there was a question specific to Gmex and

00:32:36.093 --> 00:32:41.739
since you had shared that you did a Gmex,
I'd love to direct that towards you.

00:32:41.739 --> 00:32:46.097
When doing a Gmex first,
just how easy was it to do?

00:32:46.097 --> 00:32:50.805
And if you could talk a little bit about
the support you received from the program

00:32:50.805 --> 00:32:54.961
office in terms of the team that
kind of oversees global experiences.

00:32:54.961 --> 00:33:00.741
But also,
are you able to customize your experience?

00:33:00.741 --> 00:33:04.140
How do you kind of get placed into
a company and things like that?

00:33:04.140 --> 00:33:06.630
If you could talk a little bit
more about that, that'd be great.

00:33:06.630 --> 00:33:11.261
&gt;&gt; Sure, so in terms of the customization
aspect, that's not a route that I took,

00:33:11.261 --> 00:33:13.590
though I'm not sure if it's possible.

00:33:13.590 --> 00:33:14.508
I imagine that it is,

00:33:14.508 --> 00:33:17.980
I think that there are folks who have
sourced their own genomics opportunities.

00:33:19.360 --> 00:33:22.896
But what I did is I think
what majority of people do,

00:33:22.896 --> 00:33:27.669
be career office sources a bunch of
opportunities from companies who

00:33:27.669 --> 00:33:32.750
are looking to bring GSBers in and
they'll open up a couple of spots.

00:33:32.750 --> 00:33:37.749
And there's a huge list and
I would be surprised if all of them get

00:33:37.749 --> 00:33:42.010
staffed [LAUGH] because there
seems to be more demand for

00:33:42.010 --> 00:33:45.547
GSB talent [LAUGH] than
can be fully supplied.

00:33:45.547 --> 00:33:49.222
But yeah, so,
I identified a couple of opportunities and

00:33:49.222 --> 00:33:54.022
really what you do is, at least in my
experience is, I chatted with a bunch of

00:33:54.022 --> 00:33:58.372
the people who had created those
opportunities in those companies.

00:33:58.372 --> 00:34:02.510
And you basically try to find out what's
a good fit for what they're looking for

00:34:02.510 --> 00:34:04.134
and what you're looking for.

00:34:04.134 --> 00:34:08.568
So it's partially curated
in that there's a certain

00:34:08.568 --> 00:34:12.910
amount of value that
the companies are looking for.

00:34:12.910 --> 00:34:17.719
They want you to do something for them,
it's not just you go have a fun time,

00:34:17.719 --> 00:34:20.860
you actually have to contribute something.

00:34:20.860 --> 00:34:23.720
And that's even more
rewarding because of it.

00:34:23.720 --> 00:34:27.356
So I remember I was talking
to some folks in Germany,

00:34:27.356 --> 00:34:30.423
I forget what they were
doing at this point.

00:34:30.423 --> 00:34:34.230
Buenos Aires and there was another
one too, that I spoke with.

00:34:34.230 --> 00:34:37.833
But yeah, I really clicked
with the Argentine team and

00:34:37.833 --> 00:34:42.470
because they were offering something
that was very product focused.

00:34:42.470 --> 00:34:45.487
And at that point I hadn't really
done anything in product management,

00:34:45.487 --> 00:34:48.163
my previous internship, so
I did two internships that summer.

00:34:48.163 --> 00:34:51.552
I did one in business development
at ESRI earlier in the summer,

00:34:51.552 --> 00:34:53.200
which is not product related.

00:34:53.200 --> 00:34:56.849
That was much more in my research
wheelhouse from previously.

00:34:56.849 --> 00:35:00.972
And then the product one in
Argentina really struck a chord and

00:35:00.972 --> 00:35:05.500
I was what they were looking for and
they were what I was looking for.

00:35:05.500 --> 00:35:06.895
So that's how it worked out.

00:35:06.895 --> 00:35:10.045
I don't know if there's an aspect
of the question that I missed,

00:35:10.045 --> 00:35:12.160
but I think that most
&gt;&gt; No, that's great, and

00:35:12.160 --> 00:35:15.545
I think because you didn't customize
yours that's absolutely fine, but

00:35:15.545 --> 00:35:19.266
just to answer the question there, it is
possible to customize your experience.

00:35:19.266 --> 00:35:21.649
Actually, I believe and
if you do head nods, but

00:35:21.649 --> 00:35:23.986
I believe that you can
customize not just Gmex.

00:35:23.986 --> 00:35:26.116
But let's say a global study trip,

00:35:26.116 --> 00:35:30.234
there isn't a global study trip offer
that really is kind of an area or

00:35:30.234 --> 00:35:34.778
place you want to focus on or go to, which
is hard to imagine because there's such

00:35:34.778 --> 00:35:38.625
a plethora [LAUGH] of choices each year,
but you can also design.

00:35:38.625 --> 00:35:41.071
And that's really what the global
experiences team is there for.

00:35:41.071 --> 00:35:45.560
They're part of the MBA programs office
and they're there to help you make sure

00:35:45.560 --> 00:35:49.720
that you're able to fulfill that
requirement in a way that works for you.

00:35:49.720 --> 00:35:52.827
So there are lots of
different ways to do that.

00:35:52.827 --> 00:35:56.793
One of the other questions out there, and
I'm going to drag this to each of you to

00:35:56.793 --> 00:35:59.800
share, you can share a little bit or
a lot in terms of this.

00:35:59.800 --> 00:36:03.914
But for you when you were at the GSB
what did a typical day look like?

00:36:03.914 --> 00:36:05.660
And you don't have to
walk through a whole day.

00:36:05.660 --> 00:36:10.217
But I think really the question
is getting at how do you

00:36:10.217 --> 00:36:14.076
balance studying and clubs and recruiting?

00:36:14.076 --> 00:36:18.216
And maybe what some people I always
tell from an admissions perspective and

00:36:18.216 --> 00:36:22.685
having interacted with students over the
years, it's like I think a lot of times

00:36:22.685 --> 00:36:27.171
you have to choose and prioritize because
time is definitely the biggest commodity.

00:36:27.171 --> 00:36:31.516
So maybe if you could share of
an extracurricular activity you

00:36:31.516 --> 00:36:34.104
are involved in, just in that way and

00:36:34.104 --> 00:36:38.964
as an example of perhaps highlighting
maybe how you spent your time.

00:36:38.964 --> 00:36:43.570
And this is largely outside of academics
than what were you involved in?

00:36:43.570 --> 00:36:45.720
Geraldo, why don't we start with you.

00:36:47.070 --> 00:36:53.309
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I was thinking is like really
taking us back to that time, right?

00:36:53.309 --> 00:36:57.680
Before I share the answer, I think our
class started this week in a life of.

00:36:57.680 --> 00:37:02.996
So I think it might still be online, so
I would encourage people to read that.

00:37:02.996 --> 00:37:07.976
And it really talks through a day to
day breakdown of what someone is going

00:37:07.976 --> 00:37:09.350
through.

00:37:09.350 --> 00:37:12.831
I think for me, yeah, typical day,
going back to prioritization as a product

00:37:12.831 --> 00:37:15.650
person, that's something I'm
constantly thinking about.

00:37:15.650 --> 00:37:18.194
So that's how like even
at the GSB approach, but

00:37:18.194 --> 00:37:20.000
we used to have that joke, right?

00:37:20.000 --> 00:37:23.750
You have your studies, social and
sleep, the three things and

00:37:23.750 --> 00:37:27.580
you can choose two out of those
three at a given point in time.

00:37:27.580 --> 00:37:31.360
But for me, I think going in,
having a little bit clear picture for me,

00:37:31.360 --> 00:37:34.646
socializing, meeting the people
all of that was important.

00:37:34.646 --> 00:37:39.980
And it was actually challenging myself
because I had always been so academic.

00:37:39.980 --> 00:37:43.384
So a study focused and
only worried about that growing up

00:37:43.384 --> 00:37:47.742
in an environment like that was to
actually not worry about the grades.

00:37:47.742 --> 00:37:50.879
So, not to say you should
not get good grades but

00:37:50.879 --> 00:37:55.825
it's more about what you're learning
out of it versus just ticking a box,

00:37:55.825 --> 00:37:58.749
which is what I was
trained to be in the past.

00:37:58.749 --> 00:38:03.831
So going to the question of just
how we managed the different times,

00:38:03.831 --> 00:38:08.126
I know GSB is also very good
with giving us the focus time.

00:38:08.126 --> 00:38:10.918
And it is a recruiting season and stuff or

00:38:10.918 --> 00:38:16.184
also delaying people to come on campus
company so you get the time to adjust.

00:38:16.184 --> 00:38:20.759
But discovering through
the social piece is important,

00:38:20.759 --> 00:38:24.484
I think,
in how we go about the day to day life.

00:38:24.484 --> 00:38:29.150
So yeah, definitely it's getting your
goals for the weekly or quarterly and

00:38:29.150 --> 00:38:33.339
then focusing on what you want to get
out has been more important to me.

00:38:33.339 --> 00:38:36.006
&gt;&gt; Awesome thanks, Emily could you share?

00:38:36.006 --> 00:38:40.510
&gt;&gt; Sure, I'll do second year because
second year I really had lots of fun.

00:38:40.510 --> 00:38:44.180
And I figured out like to hurls point I
figured out my priorities a little bit

00:38:44.180 --> 00:38:47.400
better first year I was sort of like
a chicken with my head cut off.

00:38:47.400 --> 00:38:49.444
I didn't really know what I wanted and
there was so

00:38:49.444 --> 00:38:51.600
much to do and I wanted to do everything.

00:38:51.600 --> 00:38:56.261
But second year, I got very into being
healthy, so I'd wake up really early and

00:38:56.261 --> 00:38:56.965
exercise.

00:38:56.965 --> 00:39:01.520
I went to ridiculous
Palo Alto exercise classes.

00:39:01.520 --> 00:39:06.990
And then there's probably, let's say
on any given day, I had like classes.

00:39:06.990 --> 00:39:09.796
So I can be totally honest,

00:39:09.796 --> 00:39:15.047
I'd cram in [LAUGH] the case
right before the class.

00:39:15.047 --> 00:39:19.168
And make sure that I had some working
knowledge of what was happening when I

00:39:19.168 --> 00:39:20.010
walked in.

00:39:20.010 --> 00:39:24.068
And honestly, the discussions and
conversations were amazing, but

00:39:24.068 --> 00:39:26.900
so there was some portion
of my day that was that.

00:39:26.900 --> 00:39:31.066
A lot of portion of my day that was
socializing in the main quad area which I

00:39:31.066 --> 00:39:34.500
don't even what was the name
Old Town Square, old square?

00:39:34.500 --> 00:39:35.363
What was it?

00:39:35.363 --> 00:39:38.850
I don't remember the name, so
no, someone needs to help me out.

00:39:38.850 --> 00:39:39.590
&gt;&gt; Town Square.

00:39:39.590 --> 00:39:43.761
&gt;&gt; It's Town Square, okay, no I was
combining Town Square with Old Town Road,

00:39:43.761 --> 00:39:44.925
I think, that song.

00:39:44.925 --> 00:39:47.713
So spending a lot of time there,
eating lunch,

00:39:47.713 --> 00:39:51.700
packing in my salad as much
there was an amazing salad deal.

00:39:51.700 --> 00:39:55.015
And then I'd probably have two or
three club meetings.

00:39:55.015 --> 00:39:57.788
I was really involved in women in
management, so I would do that for

00:39:57.788 --> 00:39:58.690
a little bit.

00:39:58.690 --> 00:40:03.450
I was involved in the retail club,
so I would do that.

00:40:03.450 --> 00:40:07.835
And then I would like hang out with
my roommates and we would plan,

00:40:07.835 --> 00:40:12.380
we hosted a lot of small group
dinners at our house, which meant like

00:40:12.380 --> 00:40:17.280
Stanford gave us money to bring people
over to our house and have dinner.

00:40:17.280 --> 00:40:21.270
And then occasionally we were like maybe
we should also do this with professors.

00:40:21.270 --> 00:40:25.960
So we brought professors and had them
come over to our house for dinner.

00:40:25.960 --> 00:40:31.617
And then Jason was reminding me there
was some fun parties that we went to,

00:40:31.617 --> 00:40:35.733
or just different activities and
leagues and stuff.

00:40:35.733 --> 00:40:40.705
So we went to, and I would probably do
that and then I would repeat the next day.

00:40:40.705 --> 00:40:43.896
And it was amazing and so much fun.

00:40:43.896 --> 00:40:46.523
So that was day in life, I think.

00:40:46.523 --> 00:40:49.092
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you, Jason.

00:40:49.092 --> 00:40:52.626
[LAUGH] She mentioned you.
[LAUGH]

00:40:52.626 --> 00:40:54.613
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I won't share data like because I

00:40:54.613 --> 00:40:57.073
was a horrible example of prioritization.

00:40:57.073 --> 00:41:01.193
And so my learning goal, you set
learning goals with your groups and

00:41:01.193 --> 00:41:05.615
week one of my learning goal that was
forced upon me was to learn to say no.

00:41:05.615 --> 00:41:10.641
Because I was, I've always been a yes
person and so I can talk about pretty much

00:41:10.641 --> 00:41:15.384
every extra curricular because I was
probably involved and I definitely out of

00:41:15.384 --> 00:41:17.960
&gt;&gt; Not just involved, you would lead it,

00:41:17.960 --> 00:41:19.764
literally lead it, yeah.

00:41:19.764 --> 00:41:23.782
&gt;&gt; And out of the three choices that are
all mentioned, I think I gave up three.

00:41:23.782 --> 00:41:28.747
So I definitely pulled way more all
nighters that I would ever recommend just

00:41:28.747 --> 00:41:33.338
to plan, the GSB show or plan a study
trip or plan something for folks.

00:41:33.338 --> 00:41:35.549
Because I love I just love
the community so much.

00:41:35.549 --> 00:41:37.480
So what I will say is,
there's something for everyone.

00:41:37.480 --> 00:41:41.352
Whether you want a club around VC or
a club around people who love media and

00:41:41.352 --> 00:41:45.099
arts or if you want to club around
robotics or honestly if you want to spend

00:41:45.099 --> 00:41:49.630
time at other schools, the law school,
the med school, there's so many choices.

00:41:49.630 --> 00:41:54.551
So what I would advise is thinking of your
framework early of what are priorities for

00:41:54.551 --> 00:41:58.205
you, which I tell startups all the time,
pick up your OKRs and

00:41:58.205 --> 00:41:59.770
don't do anything else.

00:41:59.770 --> 00:42:04.506
But think of your priorities and
what you want to say yes to and

00:42:04.506 --> 00:42:09.621
truly learn to say no to 80% of
things because there are just so

00:42:09.621 --> 00:42:13.885
many options and
it's really tough to prioritize and

00:42:13.885 --> 00:42:17.410
you don't want to spread
yourself too thin.

00:42:17.410 --> 00:42:20.620
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thanks, and John,
do you want to chime in there?

00:42:20.620 --> 00:42:22.040
&gt;&gt; Yeah, sure.

00:42:22.040 --> 00:42:25.648
I mean, big plus one [LAUGH] [INAUDIBLE]
Request prioritization [LAUGH] And

00:42:25.648 --> 00:42:27.400
figuring out what your goals are.

00:42:27.400 --> 00:42:31.810
Because there's way too much going
on to be able to do all of it.

00:42:31.810 --> 00:42:36.177
Yeah, I mean for me, yes, class is
usually going on in the mornings, but

00:42:36.177 --> 00:42:39.570
I had a couple of classes that
would go on in the evenings.

00:42:39.570 --> 00:42:45.453
And in the in between time, I spent
a bunch of time in the practice and

00:42:45.453 --> 00:42:49.080
manufacturing club as you might expect.

00:42:49.080 --> 00:42:51.282
And a lot of one on one with folks,

00:42:51.282 --> 00:42:56.486
especially people who were victim related
industries especially second year

00:42:56.486 --> 00:43:02.027
I spent a bunch of time trying to learn as
much about different companies as I could.

00:43:02.027 --> 00:43:06.774
So, that could be having coffee chat
with your professor who is a VC and

00:43:06.774 --> 00:43:10.080
invests in a couple of
these types of companies.

00:43:10.080 --> 00:43:14.196
Or maybe driving up to SF and
going and seeing someone shot and

00:43:14.196 --> 00:43:17.829
what they're doing or
going over to Sand Hill Road and

00:43:17.829 --> 00:43:23.257
being shown around by the portfolio of
companies by one of the investors there.

00:43:23.257 --> 00:43:26.197
There's just, there's a lot of that,

00:43:26.197 --> 00:43:30.952
especially second year because for
me in second year was all about

00:43:30.952 --> 00:43:36.060
trying to figure out exactly what I'm
going to do when I leave the place.

00:43:36.060 --> 00:43:39.095
But also there's a ton of socializing.

00:43:39.095 --> 00:43:44.259
I would play tennis with folks often,
multiple times a week and

00:43:44.259 --> 00:43:47.480
sometimes volunteer a little bit too.

00:43:47.480 --> 00:43:52.380
There's a program called
East Palo Alto Tennis Tutoring which I

00:43:52.380 --> 00:43:54.020
would help out with.

00:43:54.020 --> 00:43:58.100
There's basically kids would get
tutored and then they would go out and

00:43:58.100 --> 00:44:02.250
hit the tennis ball around with some
folks who have volunteered to do so.

00:44:02.250 --> 00:44:07.484
And that was, for me that was a little
bit of a grounding experience

00:44:07.484 --> 00:44:11.785
because being within the GSB
there's a lot going on and

00:44:11.785 --> 00:44:15.362
it feels like its own
world in some respects.

00:44:15.362 --> 00:44:19.463
But it's also good to get
out of it a little bit and

00:44:19.463 --> 00:44:22.975
then I loved living in my past townhouse.

00:44:22.975 --> 00:44:26.445
My housemates and
I spent a lot of time together.

00:44:26.445 --> 00:44:32.895
Whether it was having cookouts, or playing
random board games and having folks over.

00:44:32.895 --> 00:44:37.515
It was a,
that social experience was probably one of

00:44:37.515 --> 00:44:42.561
the most tight knit social
experiences that I had at GSB.

00:44:42.561 --> 00:44:49.870
And those are the people that I most
rely on [LAUGH] For advice these days.

00:44:49.870 --> 00:44:53.880
But yeah,
there's no shortage of things to do.

00:44:53.880 --> 00:44:57.620
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thanks for sharing and just
for those on the cop at pass down house.

00:44:57.620 --> 00:45:02.312
Is a term that is used within
the GSB community because

00:45:02.312 --> 00:45:06.795
many first year GSB students
will live on campus and

00:45:06.795 --> 00:45:10.350
when I say many it's in the high 90%.

00:45:10.350 --> 00:45:15.837
They'll either live in buildings that are
directly across the street from the GSB or

00:45:15.837 --> 00:45:19.090
other housing available
on the Stanford Campus.

00:45:19.090 --> 00:45:23.695
But after that in second year it is common
to move to the area around the campus and

00:45:23.695 --> 00:45:25.772
in what they call pass down houses.

00:45:25.772 --> 00:45:29.519
Houses that over the years have always
been rented to GSB students and

00:45:29.519 --> 00:45:31.340
just continue to be passed down.

00:45:31.340 --> 00:45:33.400
So that's that term.

00:45:33.400 --> 00:45:36.229
Jason earlier you had
mentioned that you actually

00:45:36.229 --> 00:45:40.133
do refer back to your what matters
most question that you answered and

00:45:40.133 --> 00:45:42.893
in your application and
we're speaking today to

00:45:42.893 --> 00:45:47.360
a bunch of people on this call who were
embarking on that application process.

00:45:47.360 --> 00:45:49.963
I'm just curious from the others,

00:45:49.963 --> 00:45:55.083
how often you might also think about
your what matters most question,

00:45:55.083 --> 00:45:59.968
and whether you use that as an anchor
point ever in your life today.

00:45:59.968 --> 00:46:03.530
Maybe Emily, [LAUGH] I see you smiling.

